<!-- ================= THEORY ================= -->

<section id="theory" class="tab-content active">

<h2 class="section-title">Complete Theory</h2>

<div class="card definition">

<h2>1. What is a Set?</h2>

<p>
A set is a well-defined collection of distinct objects.
The objects of a set are called its elements or members.
</p>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">Example</span>
A = {2, 4, 6, 8}
</div>

<p>
Here 2, 4, 6 and 8 are elements of A.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>2. Representation of Sets</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">

<h3>Roster Form</h3>

<p>Elements are listed explicitly.</p>

<div class="formula">
A = {1,2,3,4,5}
</div>

</div>

<div class="mini">

<h3>Set Builder Form</h3>

<p>Elements are represented using a condition.</p>

<div class="formula">
A = {x : x &lt; 6, x ∈ N}
</div>

</div>

<div class="mini">

<h3>Statement Form</h3>

<p>Set is described in words.</p>

<div class="formula">
Set of prime numbers less than 10
</div>

</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>3. Types of Sets</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">
<h3>Empty Set</h3>
<p>No elements.</p>
<div class="formula">∅ = { }</div>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Singleton Set</h3>
<p>Exactly one element.</p>
<div class="formula">{5}</div>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Finite Set</h3>
<p>Finite number of elements.</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Infinite Set</h3>
<p>Infinitely many elements.</p>
<div class="formula">N = {1,2,3,...}</div>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Equal Sets</h3>
<p>Exactly same elements.</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Equivalent Sets</h3>
<p>Same number of elements.</p>
<div class="formula">n(A)=n(B)</div>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Universal Set</h3>
<p>Set containing all objects under consideration.</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Disjoint Sets</h3>
<p>No common element.</p>
<div class="formula">A ∩ B = ∅</div>
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>4. Subset</h2>

<p>
A is a subset of B when every element of A belongs to B.
</p>

<div class="formula">
A ⊆ B
</div>

<div class="success">
The empty set is a subset of every set.
Also, every set is a subset of itself.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>5. Power Set</h2>

<p>
Power set of A is the set containing all subsets of A.
It is denoted by P(A).
</p>

<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example
</div>

<p>A = {1,2}</p>

<div class="formula">
P(A) = {∅,{1},{2},{1,2}}
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>6. Operations on Sets</h2>

<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Union — A ∪ B
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
Union contains elements belonging to A or B or both.
</p>

<div class="formula">
A ∪ B = {x : x ∈ A or x ∈ B}
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Intersection — A ∩ B
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
Intersection contains common elements.
</p>

<div class="formula">
A ∩ B = {x : x ∈ A and x ∈ B}
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Difference — A − B
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
Elements belonging to A but not B.
</p>

<div class="formula">
A − B = {x : x ∈ A and x ∉ B}
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="accordion">

<button onclick="toggleAccordion(this)">
Complement — A'
</button>

<div class="accordion-content">

<p>
Elements of U which do not belong to A.
</p>

<div class="formula">
A' = U − A
</div>

</div>

</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>7. Cartesian Product</h2>

<p>
Cartesian product A × B is the set of all ordered pairs (a,b).
</p>

<div class="formula">
A × B = {(a,b) : a ∈ A, b ∈ B}
</div>

<div class="formula">
n(A × B) = n(A) × n(B)
</div>

<div class="warning">
Order matters:
<strong>(a,b) ≠ (b,a)</strong> in general.
</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- ================= FORMULAS ================= -->

<section id="formulas" class="tab-content">

<h2 class="section-title">Complete Formula Sheet</h2>


<div class="card">

<h2>Subset Formulas</h2>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">Total Subsets</span>
2ⁿ
</div>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">Proper Subsets</span>
2ⁿ − 1
</div>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">Non-empty Subsets</span>
2ⁿ − 1
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>Two Set Formula</h2>

<div class="formula">
n(A ∪ B)
=
n(A)+n(B)−n(A∩B)
</div>

<div class="formula">
n(A only)
=
n(A)−n(A∩B)
</div>

<div class="formula">
n(B only)
=
n(B)−n(A∩B)
</div>

<div class="formula">
Neither
=
n(U)−n(A∪B)
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>Three Set Formula</h2>

<div class="formula">
n(A∪B∪C)
=
n(A)+n(B)+n(C)
−n(A∩B)
−n(B∩C)
−n(C∩A)
+n(A∩B∩C)
</div>

<div class="formula">
Only A
=
A−AB−AC+ABC
</div>

<div class="formula">
Only B
=
B−AB−BC+ABC
</div>

<div class="formula">
Only C
=
C−AC−BC+ABC
</div>

<div class="formula">
Exactly Two
=
AB+BC+CA−3ABC
</div>

<div class="formula">
None
=
U−(A∪B∪C)
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>Complement Formulas</h2>

<div class="formula">
A ∪ A' = U
</div>

<div class="formula">
A ∩ A' = ∅
</div>

<div class="formula">
(A')' = A
</div>

<div class="formula">
n(A') = n(U)−n(A)
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>De Morgan's Laws</h2>

<div class="formula">
(A∪B)' = A'∩B'
</div>

<div class="formula">
(A∩B)' = A'∪B'
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>Important Identities</h2>

<div class="table-wrap">

<table>

<tr>
<th>Law</th>
<th>Identity</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Commutative</td>
<td>A∪B = B∪A</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Commutative</td>
<td>A∩B = B∩A</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Associative</td>
<td>(A∪B)∪C = A∪(B∪C)</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Associative</td>
<td>(A∩B)∩C = A∩(B∩C)</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Identity</td>
<td>A∪∅ = A</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Identity</td>
<td>A∩U = A</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Domination</td>
<td>A∪U = U</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Domination</td>
<td>A∩∅ = ∅</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Idempotent</td>
<td>A∪A = A</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Idempotent</td>
<td>A∩A = A</td>
</tr>

</table>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- ================= EXAMPLES ================= -->

<section id="examples" class="tab-content">

<h2 class="section-title">Solved Examples</h2>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 1 — Number of Subsets
</div>

<p>
A set contains 5 elements. Find the number of subsets.
</p>

<div class="formula">
2⁵ = 32
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>32</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 2 — Proper Subsets
</div>

<p>
A set contains 6 elements. Find its proper subsets.
</p>

<div class="formula">
2⁶ − 1 = 63
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>63</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 3 — Union
</div>

<p>
n(A)=35, n(B)=28 and n(A∩B)=12.
Find n(A∪B).
</p>

<div class="formula">
35 + 28 − 12 = 51
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>51</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 4 — Neither
</div>

<p>
There are 80 students. 45 study Maths,
40 study Physics and 20 study both.
Find students studying neither.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Maths ∪ Physics
=
45+40−20
=
65
</div>

<div class="formula">
Neither = 80−65 = 15
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>15 students</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 5 — Three Sets
</div>

<p>
A=40, B=35, C=30,
AB=15, BC=10, CA=12 and ABC=5.
Find A∪B∪C.
</p>

<div class="formula">
40+35+30−15−10−12+5
=
73
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>73</strong>
</div>

</div>


<div class="example">

<div class="example-title">
Example 6 — Cartesian Product
</div>

<p>
If n(A)=4 and n(B)=7, find n(A×B).
</p>

<div class="formula">
4×7 = 28
</div>

<div class="answer">
Answer: <strong>28</strong>
</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- ================= TRICKS ================= -->

<section id="tricks" class="tab-content">

<h2 class="section-title">Short Tricks & Exam Tips</h2>


<div class="card">

<h2>🔥 Trick 1 — Subset Question</h2>

<p>
Whenever the question gives number of elements directly,
immediately use:
</p>

<div class="formula">
n elements → 2ⁿ subsets
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>🔥 Trick 2 — Proper Subsets</h2>

<div class="formula">
Proper subsets = 2ⁿ−1
</div>

<p>
Why minus 1? Because the set itself is removed.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>🔥 Trick 3 — At Least One</h2>

<div class="formula">
At least one = Total − None
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>🔥 Trick 4 — Neither</h2>

<div class="formula">
Neither = Total − Union
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>🔥 Trick 5 — Two Sets</h2>

<div class="formula">
Union = A+B−AB
</div>

<div class="warning">
Never add A+B directly when the sets overlap.
The common part must be subtracted once.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>🔥 Trick 6 — Three Sets</h2>

<div class="formula">
Union
=
Singles − Pairwise + Triple
</div>

<p>
Think:
<strong>+</strong> individual sets → <strong>−</strong> pair intersections →
<strong>+</strong> triple intersection.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>⚠️ Common Exam Traps</h2>

<ul>

<li>
Repeated elements are counted only once.
</li>

<li>
Order of elements does not matter.
</li>

<li>
A set is always a subset of itself.
</li>

<li>
∅ is a subset of every set.
</li>

<li>
A−B is generally not equal to B−A.
</li>

<li>
A×B is generally not equal to B×A.
</li>

<li>
Do not forget the intersection while calculating union.
</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>


